tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:1: # iolbox-launcher
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:2: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:3: A single Windows exe that boots the iolbox Linux guest and gets a user to
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:4: `http://localhost:4001`. Picks a backend automatically:
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:5: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:6: - **qemu** â€” bundled `qemu-system-x86_64.exe` (TCG software emulation). Works
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:7:   everywhere, conflicts with nothing, needs no admin. The fallback.
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:8: - **wsl** â€” the `iolbox` WSL2 distro. Fastest, but only usable when an active
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:9:   Windows hypervisor is present (see `docs/providers.md`).
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:10: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:11: Build (dependency-free, stdlib only):
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:12: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:13: ```
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:14: cd tools/iolab-launcher
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:15: GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build -o iolbox-launcher.exe .
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:16: ```
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:17: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:18: ## Ephemeral OS disk
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:19: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:20: The qemu backend attaches `iolbox-disk.qcow2` with `snapshot=on`. QEMU opens
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:21: the golden disk **read-only** and redirects every guest write to a temporary
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:22: overlay file that is discarded when qemu exits. That means:
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:23: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:24: - A hard kill (task-kill, crash, power loss) can **never corrupt** the shipped
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:25:   disk image.
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:26: - Every launch boots from the exact same clean state.
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:27: - Nothing written to the guest's root filesystem persists between runs.
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:28: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:29: (If qemu is hard-killed before it can clean up, its temp overlay file may be
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:30: orphaned in `%TMP%` â€” this is harmless scratch space; Windows/the user can
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:31: clean `%TMP%` at any time.)
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:32: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:33: The WSL backend has no qcow2 â€” its distro filesystem already persists on the
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:34: Windows FS across runs, so this concern doesn't apply there.
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:35: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:36: ## images\ and labs\ folders
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:37: 
tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:38: Because the OS disk is ephemeral, user data lives on the Windows filesystem
