+| 6 | **MAJOR** | “Load stays fine and only start rejects” is broadly true for a stopped document, but incomplete for a running lab. Already-running IOL nodes bypass `buildSpec`; Restart stops first and then would reject. A same-topology `lab.load` can adopt the running lab. | `lab.load` validates and records image IDs but does not inspect arch (`supervisor/internal/server/handlers.go:106-170`); it adopts a matching running lab at `handlers.go:136-149`. `startNodes` returns an already-running process before `buildSpec` (`handlers.go:620-627`). `node.restart` synchronously stops before calling Start (`handlers.go:543-559`). Autosave stores raw document text without image-arch policy (`supervisor/internal/server/labstore.go:58-86`). | Document exact behavior: saved/autosaved docs and stopped `lab.load` remain valid; a stopped i386 node rejects on Start; an already-running node is returned as running by Start; Restart stops it and then rejects; loading a different lab follows the pre-existing teardown behavior. Add tests for stopped, already-running, restart, same-lab adoption, and multi-node partial failure if enforcement stays.
+| 7 | **MAJOR** | Putting rejection only in `buildSpec` is too late to be a clean preflight. Capture arming, fabric refresh, and lab-directory preparation already happen before `buildSpec`. | `startNodes` arms captures, refreshes fabric, and calls `prepareLabDir` at `supervisor/internal/server/handlers.go:568-598`; `buildSpec` is not reached until `handlers.go:693-703`. | If server enforcement stays, validate all targeted IOL image architectures before `armDocCaptures`/`refreshFabric`/`prepareLabDir`. Preserve existing multi-node partial-failure behavior deliberately; do not accidentally turn one bad image into a whole-request top-level error.
+| 8 | **NOTE** | The feared “ordinary launcher start never rewrites an M1–M4 drop-in” hole does **not** exist in the current launcher. Both `start` and `upgrade` run the complete guest sequence, including `40-install-payload.sh`, which atomically rewrites the same drop-in. A mixed old-assets/new-payload invocation or raw `limactl start` can still run without the new environment until the M5 launcher provisions it. | CLI sends both commands to `runProvision` (`tools/iolab-launcher/macos_cli.go:153-202`). `runProvision` always stages files and calls `runGuestSequence` (`macos_lifecycle.go:349-354`); that sequence always runs `30-canary.sh`, `40-install-payload.sh`, and `50-verify.sh` (`macos_lifecycle.go:272-288`). The installer preserves drop-ins because it installs the base unit then starts it (`runtime/files/native/install.sh:250-291`); `40-install-payload.sh` rewrites the drop-in before invoking it (`packaging/macos/guest/40-install-payload.sh:197-213`). | Keep fail-open for non-Mac compatibility. Write `Environment=IOLBOX_DISABLE_I386=1` into the existing atomic drop-in, verify effective `Environment` plus correlated Hello in 40/50, and couple the M5 payload to M5 assets in staging/evidence. State that direct Lima starts or old asset bundles are outside the M5 launcher readiness contract; diagnostics must flag that drift rather than silently print healthy capability policy.
