+| 8 | **NOTE** | The feared “ordinary launcher start never rewrites an M1–M4 drop-in” hole does **not** exist in the current launcher. Both `start` and `upgrade` run the complete guest sequence, including `40-install-payload.sh`, which atomically rewrites the same drop-in. A mixed old-assets/new-payload invocation or raw `limactl start` can still run without the new environment until the M5 launcher provisions it. | CLI sends both commands to `runProvision` (`tools/iolab-launcher/macos_cli.go:153-202`). `runProvision` always stages files and calls `runGuestSequence` (`macos_lifecycle.go:349-354`); that sequence always runs `30-canary.sh`, `40-install-payload.sh`, and `50-verify.sh` (`macos_lifecycle.go:272-288`). The installer preserves drop-ins because it installs the base unit then starts it (`runtime/files/native/install.sh:250-291`); `40-install-payload.sh` rewrites the drop-in before invoking it (`packaging/macos/guest/40-install-payload.sh:197-213`). | Keep fail-open for non-Mac compatibility. Write `Environment=IOLBOX_DISABLE_I386=1` into the existing atomic drop-in, verify effective `Environment` plus correlated Hello in 40/50, and couple the M5 payload to M5 assets in staging/evidence. State that direct Lima starts or old asset bundles are outside the M5 launcher readiness contract; diagnostics must flag that drift rather than silently print healthy capability policy.
+| 9 | **MAJOR** | Supervisor service/Hello reachability is not a sound predicate for `execution=rosetta-amd64`. A crashed supervisor does not change the guest's execution mode. Conversely, a reachable service is merely an amd64 workload observation already subsumed by the executable canary. | The current diagnose path separately runs the live canary and separately probes service/Hello (`tools/iolab-launcher/macos_cli.go:307-335`). The canary deliberately says the executable test is authoritative and binfmt is diagnostic context (`packaging/macos/guest/30-canary.sh:63-83`). | Derive execution from: live guest `uname -m == aarch64`; enabled live Rosetta binfmt entry naming `/mnt/lima-rosetta/rosetta`; and a **live** passing amd64 loader canary (or a record proven current for the same kernel/binfmt, if live execution cannot be repeated). Report service, HTTP, Hello, and capability policy as separate health predicates. A crashed supervisor may yield `execution=rosetta-amd64` plus `service=FAIL`.
+| 10 | **MINOR** | §6 blurs the executable canary record and the structural attestation. Their field names differ, and the real canary verdict has four named failure values, not merely PASS/non-PASS. | `30-canary.sh` emits `PASS`, `FAIL_AUXV`, `FAIL_MISSING`, `FAIL_NOEXEC`, or `FAIL_OTHER` (`packaging/macos/guest/30-canary.sh:47-60`). Its JSON renderer is at `30-canary.sh:176-203`, and writes `/var/lib/iolbox/macos-canary.json` at `30-canary.sh:212-214`. The structural attestation instead uses `canary_verdict` (`packaging/macos/guest/40-install-payload.sh:134-150`). | Parse the executable record as exactly: `{"schema":1,"macos_product":"...","macos_build":"...","lima_version":"...","profile":"...","kernel":"...","binfmt":"...","verdict":"PASS|FAIL_AUXV|FAIL_MISSING|FAIL_NOEXEC|FAIL_OTHER","timestamp":"YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ","version":"...","error":"..."}`. Label host `macos-structural-gate.json` values as last structural attestation, never as the live canary.
+| 11 | **MAJOR** | §11's claim that the M4 handoff marks `iolbox-m4-e2e` reusable is factually wrong. Reuse will not erase the sealed evidence tree, but it replaces the supervisor/configuration and removes the easiest environment for unfinished M4 reconfirmation. | The M4 result calls it the “M4 working machine,” with the fixed build and both images (`docs/macos-m4-result.md:203-210`); the M4 handoff contains no “reusable” designation. M4 remains PARTIAL (`docs/macos-m4-result.md:34-41`). | Prefer a fresh `iolbox-m5-e2e`; copy/register the known router image and use the explicit M5 payload. If schedule forces reuse, first capture current version, effective unit, machine config, image list, and state, and acknowledge that the machine—not the immutable evidence directories—will cease to be the M4 working baseline.
+| 12 | **MAJOR** | The rendered-browser proof is feasible through the proposed SSH tunnel, but a Bash harness alone cannot honestly claim DOM assertions/screenshots without a specified interactive or automation step. The M3 cookie/origin defect does **not** invalidate the tunnel. | SPA GET sets a Strict, path-wide `iolbox_session` cookie (`supervisor/internal/wsbridge/wsbridge.go:182-193`). Every WS route requires that cookie and exact Origin host/port equality (`wsbridge.go:196-237`). The GUI builds `/control` from `location.host` (`app/src/lib/transportSelect.ts:15-17,31`), so at `http://127.0.0.1:44001/` both WS `Origin` and HTTP `Host` remain `127.0.0.1:44001` through `ssh -L`; they match. M3 records the cookie/origin requirement (`docs/macos-m3-handoff.md:42-46`). | Keep the tunnel. Split step 5 into a machine-side fixture/setup phase and an explicitly manual or browser-automation phase. First load `/` to mint the cookie, then use same-origin WS. Record who/what captured DOM properties and screenshots; do not let `hardware-m5.sh` mark the browser criterion PASS merely because setup succeeded.
+| 13 | **MINOR** | The plan's router/switch concern resolves in favor of the command it shows: the router path is real and is what `hardware-m1.sh` needs. The phase name and flags are exact. | `hardware-m1.sh` documents and accepts `--phase install-image-and-lab`, `--machine`, and `--image` (`packaging/macos/tests/hardware-m1.sh:25-40,647-685`). It creates an L3 two-router lab (`hardware-m1.sh:367-399,436-450`). The actual Mac router path is recorded at `docs/macos-m1-handoff.md:107-111` and `docs/macos-m3-handoff.md:146-150`. M4's similarly named `_l2-` file is explicitly the switch (`docs/macos-m4-result.md:123-126`). | Keep `~/iolbox-m0/x86_64_crb_linux-adventerprisek9-ms.iol17.18.02.bin` for step 6. Do not substitute the `_l2-` switch image; the harness hardcodes router configuration and class.
+| 14 | **MAJOR** | §10's “must splice in the Linux guest” and open dry-run risk are obsolete. `runtime/pack-native.sh` cannot be used on this Windows/J: worktree, but the focused splice can be made on macOS. | The guest installer uses ordinary `tar -xzf`, then explicitly requires executable `install.sh` (`packaging/macos/guest/40-install-payload.sh:215-242`). The packer itself requires executable VPCS at preflight (`runtime/pack-native.sh:123-133`) and normally archives POSIX modes (`runtime/pack-native.sh:295-303`). `build-release.sh` already creates the GUI-embedded Linux/amd64 supervisor (`build-release.sh:18-33`). | Replace §10 with the completed path: build `supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-amd64` through `build-release.sh` on Windows; scp it to the Mac; extract a copy of the known-good v0.5.3 tree; replace only `bin/supervisor`; `chmod 0755`; retar on macOS (prefer `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1` to avoid AppleDouble metadata). Record the existing probe `~/iolbox-m0/iolbox-server-v0.5.4-m5-probe.tar.gz`, sha256 `804aab32718d8f072bc2c38e135a6ec18a83e79c011a2df25c56754e0d0da022`. BSD tar's pax/ustar output is readable by GNU tar; proof is `tar -tzvf` mode inspection plus guest `tar -xzf`, `test -x .../install.sh .../bin/supervisor`, `bin/supervisor --version`, and a successful launcher upgrade. The splice does not need to occur inside the Linux guest.
+| 15 | **NOTE** | The plan does not need to update every repository mention of i386. The two queried references accurately describe non-Mac targets. The launcher README does exist, although its opening description is still Windows-only and is a valid M5 documentation target. | `docs/providers.md:77-93` scopes `libc6:i386` to the VMware/WSL rootfs. `docker/README.md:150-177` scopes i386 support to Docker. `tools/iolab-launcher/README.md:1-15` exists and describes a Windows executable; it later contains the Mac harness (`README.md:70-80`). | Do not edit `docs/providers.md` or `docker/README.md` merely to add a Mac exception to correctly scoped non-Mac statements. Update `docs/INSTALL.md` and the launcher README with Apple-Silicon x86_64-IOL-only behavior and measured diagnostic keys, as the plan already proposes.
+| 16 | **NOTE** | No existing test was found that asserts an exact `features` array shape, and the default mock will not break. The plan should still pin default omission/feature order because it claims byte compatibility. | Existing server tests check membership, not exact equality (`supervisor/internal/server/extnet_test.go:14-46`; `server_test.go:154-171`). Protocol serialization only checks required key presence (`supervisor/internal/protocol/protocol_test.go:105-110`). The mock Hello contains `i386` (`app/src/lib/mockTransport.ts:185-196`). | Leave the default mock unchanged. Add focused tests for default feature order and Mac omission; if the positive field is retained, test its default omission. A separate Mac mock fixture is optional and should only be added if a component/store test actually consumes it.
+
+## Hardware sequence check
+
+1. **Environment/input recording — runnable.** All facts are available through `sw_vers`, `uname`, `limactl`, tar listing, hashes, and guest execution. Execute the Linux supervisor version check in the guest, not on macOS arm64.
+2. **Explicit launcher upgrade — runnable.** `upgrade` already provisions, verifies, waits for HTTP, and checks WS Hello. A following `start` is normally redundant and would reinstall/reverify the same payload again (`tools/iolab-launcher/macos_lifecycle.go:349-369`).
+3. **Correlated Hello — runnable.** The raw control port is deliberately not host-forwarded (`tools/iolab-launcher/macos_cli.go:246-265`), so capture it through `limactl shell` on guest `127.0.0.1:4000` or through the authenticated host WS client, correlating by request ID as M1 requires (`docs/macos-m1-handoff.md:147-150`).
+4. **Status/diagnose and stopped-state check — runnable after implementation.** Compare the four summary keys with independent guest facts; stop only after live facts have been captured.
+5. **Synthetic ELF32 + rendered GUI — runnable with the split manual/automation qualification in finding 12.** A >1 KiB ELF32/EM_386 header classifies i386; `image.ParseArch` reads EI_CLASS/e_machine (`supervisor/internal/image/image.go:108-135`), and files over 1 KiB receive a usable class (`image.go:181-195`). The SSH tunnel preserves cookie and same-origin semantics.
+6. **M1 real x86_64 lab — runnable with the exact command shown.** The phase and flags exist, and the non-`_l2-` router path is correct. The harness asserts executable mode before registering (`packaging/macos/tests/hardware-m1.sh:322-365`) and inspects `result.failed[]` rather than trusting `ok:true` (`hardware-m1.sh:436-452`).
+7. **Stop/seal/summary — runnable.** Keep the browser step explicitly PASS/FAIL/NOT RUN separate from the shell setup so the final summary cannot round a fixture-only run up to GUI PASS.
+
+## Corrections to fold into the plan
+
+1. Remove `iolArchitectures` from the minimum M5 design and use `features.includes("i386")` as the sole image-architecture support signal. If retained by owner decision, mark it optional, keep lower-camel spelling, nil-by-default `omitempty`, and add an exact default JSON test.
+2. Add `TopBar.svelte` and both App/Top Bar lab-start buttons to §4 and §8. Expose one store-derived lab unsupported reason so all buttons use identical logic and wording.
+3. Keep the six named image/start components in scope, but trim nonessential embellishments: no changed default mock; data hooks only where the browser proof uses them; Image Manager's critical rule is unsupported images allowed in **From** and disabled/excluded in **To**.
+4. Recast server `image_arch_mismatch` as an explicit optional behavior change. If kept, run architecture validation before capture/fabric/lab preparation and specify stopped, already-running, Restart, adopt-load, and bulk-partial semantics.
+5. State the actual blast radius: no-signal non-Mac paths are unchanged; any deployment explicitly setting `IOLBOX_DISABLE_I386=1` loses i386 starts by design. The new GUI's feature-only logic preserves all known old supervisors because they already advertise `i386`.
+6. Keep fail-open, but make M5 asset/payload pairing explicit. Atomically add the environment line to the existing drop-in; verify effective `Environment`, `ExecStartPre`, correlated Hello omission, and readiness on both launcher `start` and `upgrade`.
+7. Derive `execution=rosetta-amd64` from live arch + enabled Rosetta binfmt/interpreter + live passing loader canary. Remove supervisor service/Hello from execution derivation and report it separately as health/capability policy.
+8. Parse `/var/lib/iolbox/macos-canary.json` with the exact record shape and verdict vocabulary above. Do not confuse its `verdict` with structural attestation `canary_verdict`.
+9. Replace §10 with the already-proven Windows build/macOS splice workflow and recorded probe hash. Add tar member-mode and guest extraction/execution proof; do not require the splice to happen inside Linux.
+10. Prefer `iolbox-m5-e2e`. If reusing `iolbox-m4-e2e`, snapshot its current operational facts first and stop claiming the M4 handoff explicitly marked it reusable.
+11. Keep the router path and exact M1 phase. Remove any suggestion that the `_l2-` switch image is interchangeable for the two-router harness.
+12. Split browser verification from the Bash harness, document the browser/automation operator, load `/` first for the session cookie, and use the same tunneled origin for all WS routes.
+13. Leave `docs/providers.md`, `docker/README.md`, and the default mock alone. Update `docs/protocol.md`, `docs/INSTALL.md`, and `tools/iolab-launcher/README.md` as applicable to the final chosen protocol.
+14. Reduce §9 to tests that pin the actual contract: environment parser; default/Mac Hello; central GUI helper and store guards; drop-in/effective-unit/Hello verification; measured diagnostics; existing full suites. Keep one rendered-browser hardware pass and the real x86_64 lab pass; the new umbrella harness is optional.
+
+## Questions only the owner can answer
+
+1. Which real ordinary amd64 non-Mac target should supply criterion 2 evidence? The repository cannot identify or authorize an external machine, and the immutable prompt requires real-hardware proof.
+2. Is server-side start rejection an intended M5 product behavior, or should M5 remain the smallest reporting/UI change? It is defensible safety hardening, but it is not one of the five explicit observable criteria and no current path emits that error.
+3. Does the owner want `iolArchitectures` as a deliberate future-facing protocol contract despite its being unnecessary for all known old/new supervisors? If not, it should be removed rather than allowed to consume the milestone by default.

